// TERMINAL VELOCITY — ENDSTATION — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// TERMINAL VELOCITY as Kraftwerk, circa The Man-Machine and Computer World — a game
// alternate of neon, selectable from the dev menu (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the desk.
// Endstation is German for terminus.
// 
// A station melody played by the machines that run the line: the Seseragi jingle on
// a plain square with the cabinet's alloy chime over it, the Tron sixteenths on the
// sequencer, a Minimoog on octaves, dry electronic drums (Simmons snare, metal hats),
// Kraftwerk blips and the Casio pi-po, a Polymoog choir and brass, a vocoder on the
// second theme, and a robot announcer on six bits calling the stops. No risers and no
// pump: precision, repetition, parts entering one at a time.
// 
// NEON'S TIMING, kept (src/engine/stylePacks/neonMoods.js): the minor turn is the
// downbeat of bar 15 (beat 56) and the lift to D minor the downbeat of bar 45 (beat
// 176) — neon's two lightning strikes. The cabinet screen loops bars 3–6, which puts
// the turn about 15 s into the level (neon-1's first rideable train wants 13–18 s).
// The game loop returns to bar 15.
// 
//   1–2    the announcer, "terminal velocity"; the chime's sweep
//   3–6    the jingle, C C G G (the cabinet-screen loop)
//   7–13   the kit; the whole jingle and its cadence; "next station"
//   14     the minor sweep and a fill
//   15–22  THE TURN (beat 56): C minor, the Tron sequencer, choir, the figure minor
//   23–30  the second theme on the vocoder, Polymoog brass, the ostinato
//   31–38  the calculator, then Shibuya on chime and Rhodes: "next station Shibuya"
//   39–44  the figure minor again, parts returning; the sweep
//   45–60  D MINOR (beat 176): the figure and the second theme, everything
//   61–72  the arrival: C major, the jingle bright; the machine winds down
// 
// Every melody is SESERAGI v17's (JR East copyright: if the game ships with it, it
// needs clearing — the caveat carries over from neon.js). The announcer lines are this
// song's own voices (voiceParams), compiled from text by work/local/_kw-speak.mjs.
// 
// 72 bars at 124. Written by work/local/_neon-remix-endstation.mjs through
// work/local/_remix-lib.mjs; re-running it rewrites this file.
//
// SPOT FX — LIGHTNING (1 Oct 2026, work/local/_spot-fx-picture.mjs): a sixteenth of the whole mix
// overdriven and crushed on bar 45's downbeat, the strike at beat 176 the game flashes every pass.
//
// SPOT FX — MORE (1 Oct 2026, work/local/_spot-fx-more.mjs): the machine winds down: each part stops on a cut (61–72), and the last bar loops itself quieter and quieter.
//
// The music below is the composition. Everything under THE DESK WRITES BELOW HERE
// is written by `npm run mixer` and will be rewritten on every save — put notes
// about the song up here, where they survive.
